Title: Allow cross-building Windows binaries from a Linux host
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Applied from src/ after the chromium/, electron/ and node/ series, so it can
adjust code those add.

Chromium cross builds compile some targets twice: once with the host toolchain
(Linux) for build-time tools, once with the target toolchain (Windows) for what
ships. Several imported patches restore Windows-only code into files that build
on every platform, which is invisible when the host is Windows and a compile
error when it is not:

  base/files/file_util.cc        chromium/base/0324 -- upstream changed
                                 file_pre_reader_win.cc, a Windows-only file;
                                 ours targets file_util.cc. Guards the restored
                                 code and makes the XP path an early return.
  net/base/ip_endpoint.cc        chromium/net/0070 -- resolves if_indextoname
                                 from Iphlpapi.dll because XP lacks it.
  .../quiche/quiche_ip_address.cc chromium/net/0251 -- inet_ntop/inet_pton
                                 replacements for XP, reading the Win32 in_addr
                                 layout (S_un). Uses quiche's own
                                 #if defined(_WIN32), as quiche does not use
                                 Chromium's BUILDFLAG.

  third_party/libxml/src/threads.c
                                 chromium/third_party/libxml/0082 -- a
                                 fake_pthread_once standing in for Vista+
                                 InitOnceExecuteOnce, using Interlocked* and
                                 SwitchToThread. Guarded with
                                 HAVE_WIN32_THREADS, libxml's own idiom.
                                 That patch also retyped onceControl from
                                 INIT_ONCE to volatile long but left
                                 xmlCleanupParser copying an INIT_ONCE over it,
                                 writing 8 bytes into 4 on x64 (clang
                                 -Wfortify-source catches it). That reset is now
                                 a plain onceControl = 0, which is what
                                 fake_pthread_once reads as "not yet run"; it
                                 affects Windows builds too, not just cross
                                 builds.

  ui/base/l10n/l10n_util.cc      chromium/ui/0284 -- an IsFontPresent using GDI
                                 (CreateFont/GetDC/GetTextFace) plus two
                                 base::win::GetVersion() checks that block
                                 Amharic on XP without the Abyssinica SIL font.
                                 Note win/windows_version.cc is inside
                                 if (is_win) in base/BUILD.gn, so those checks
                                 would have failed at link rather than compile
                                 even once the GDI code built; the include moved
                                 into the file's existing IS_WIN block.
  ui/base/pointer/touch_ui_controller.cc
                                 chromium/ui/0174 -- added a
                                 base::win::Version::WIN10 gate to the
                                 constructor, while putting its
                                 base/win/windows_version.h include inside the
                                 existing IS_WIN block. Other platforms keep
                                 upstream's condition.

  third_party/xnnpack/src/src/xnnpack/init-once.c
                                 chromium/third_party/xnnpack/0009 -- pastes in
                                 Microsoft's NT pushlock.c (EX_PUSH_LOCK,
                                 KeRaiseIrql, InterlockedCompareExchangePointer)
                                 as a stand-in for Vista+ InitOnceExecuteOnce,
                                 ~530 lines with no guard at all. Wrapped in
                                 XNN_PLATFORM_WINDOWS, which is what the
                                 xnn_init_once_impl() call sites below it
                                 already use (common.h defines it from _WIN32).

  v8/src/api/api.cc              chromium/v8/0072 -- adds an #else stub for
                                 Isolate::SetFilterETWSessionByURL2Callback so
                                 Windows builds without
                                 V8_ENABLE_ETW_STACK_WALKING still define it.
                                 Upstream has no #else at all, and the
                                 FilterETWSessionByURL2Callback type plus the
                                 declaration are both inside V8_OS_WIN
                                 (v8-callbacks.h, v8-isolate.h), so off Windows
                                 the stub names a type that does not exist. Now
                                 an #elif defined(V8_OS_WIN).

  device/bluetooth/bluetooth_adapter_factory.cc
                                 chromium/device/0187 -- replaced upstream's
                                 compile-time platform list for
                                 kBluetoothSupportedByPlatform with an
                                 unconditional base::win::GetVersion() >= WIN10
                                 check, which neither compiles nor links off
                                 Windows. The Win10 gate is now under
                                 BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN) with upstream's list kept
                                 for the other platforms.

  third_party/blink/.../font_cache.cc
                                 chromium/third_party/blink/0309 replaced
                                 upstream's `FontCache::FontCache() = default`
                                 with a body touching s_useSubpixelPositioning,
                                 and 0311 extended it to s_useDirectWrite --
                                 both statics are declared and defined under
                                 BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN), the constructor body was
                                 not. Guarded to match. 0311 also left an empty
                                 `if (!s_useDirectWrite) {}` behind where the
                                 GDI branch used to be; removed.

  third_party/blink/.../skia/font_cache_skia.cc
                                 two separate breakages. chromium/third_party/
                                 blink/0311 added an unguarded
                                 `if (!useDirectWrite())` GDI fallback, but
                                 FontCache::useDirectWrite() is declared under
                                 BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN) while that function compiles
                                 everywhere. And 0309 appended
                                 `fontDescription.useSubpixelPositioning()` as a
                                 9th FontPlatformData argument -- a typo for
                                 font_description, and useSubpixelPositioning()
                                 is a FontCache member (also IS_WIN-only), not a
                                 FontDescription one. That call sits inside
                                 !BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN), so it had never compiled on
                                 any platform: excluded on Windows, broken
                                 elsewhere. The argument is dropped, which
                                 restores upstream behaviour -- the parameter
                                 defaults to defaultUseSubpixelPositioning(),
                                 and Windows has its own CreateFontPlatformData
                                 in fonts/win/font_cache_skia_win.cc.

  third_party/blink/.../font_platform_data.h
                                 that default argument is itself the next
                                 problem, and it is a link error rather than a
                                 compile one: 0309 added the parameter and its
                                 `= defaultUseSubpixelPositioning()` default
                                 unconditionally, but only defined the function
                                 in fonts/win/font_platform_data_win.cc, where
                                 it returns FontCache::useSubpixelPositioning()
                                 -- IS_WIN-only in turn. Off Windows every call
                                 that leaves the argument out emits a reference
                                 to a symbol nothing defines, which took down
                                 the host-toolchain link of
                                 v8_context_snapshot_generator (from
                                 font_cache_skia.cc and from this class's own
                                 (const FontPlatformData&, float) constructor).
                                 The default is false off Windows, and the
                                 declaration moves inside the IS_WIN block next
                                 to the only code that can satisfy it. Nothing
                                 reads the m_useSubpixelPositioning the
                                 parameter feeds -- 0309 writes it in both
                                 constructors and never uses it -- so this only
                                 changes which value that dead member holds; the
                                 live use, QuerySystemForRenderStyle(), is
                                 Windows-only and keeps the real default.

A second class of host-only breakage is filename case. chromium/sandbox/0319
restores the Wow64 helper as src/Wow64.cc, src/Wow64.h and src/Wow64_64.cc,
and lists those names in sandbox/win/BUILD.gn, but two of the #includes it adds
spell the header "sandbox/win/src/wow64.h" (Chromium's own naming, and what the
file was called before upstream deleted it). NTFS does not care; a Linux host
does, and both files fail with "'sandbox/win/src/wow64.h' file not found":

  sandbox/win/src/interception.cc
  sandbox/win/src/Wow64_64.cc    both includes now match the filename 0319
                                 actually creates. Renaming the three files to
                                 lowercase instead would match Chromium style,
                                 but keeps diverging from the imported patch
                                 every time it is re-imported.

It also fixes one Electron target that cannot work in a cross build at all:
mksnapshot_checksum_gen hashes the v8 context snapshot, but Chromium disables
that snapshot for Windows and macOS cross builds (building Blink twice is slow,
see tools/v8_context_snapshot/v8_context_snapshot.gni). The target was defined
whenever current_toolchain == default_toolchain while only being depended on
under the full condition, and GN validates every defined target, so gn gen
failed with "Input to target not generated by a dependency". Both now use one
enable_v8_context_snapshot_checksum flag, and the two places that read
snapshot_checksum.h are guarded by it. Note release builds set
use_v8_context_snapshot = true explicitly (see assets/args), which turns the
checksum back on and accepts the second Blink build.

